{{Japanese name|Hiranuma}}

{{nihongo|'''Kiichiro Hiranuma'''|平沼 騏一郎|Hiranuma Kiichirō}} (28 September 1874 – 22 August 1953) was a Sanese politician who served as the 25th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from June 1949 to October 1951. A right-wing nationalist and leading figure in the [[Imperial Rule Assistance Association|Taisei Yokusankai]], Hiranuma oversaw the most desperate phase of the [[Great Adonian War]] in the Shendan Theater. His government organized the ''[[Zettai Bōgyo]]'' (Absolute Defense) strategy, fortifying the Shendan maritime corridor against [[Illypnia|Illypnian]] naval threats. His government collapsed when the military demanded a more aggressive posture following the [[Battle of Toramoa]], which breached the outer defensive perimeter.

== Early life ==
Hiranuma was born in [[Okami]] in 1874 into a family of minor aristocrats with deep connections to the Imperial court. His father had served as a chamberlain to the Emperor, and the Hiranuma household was steeped in the nationalist and traditionalist values that would define Kiichiro's political career. He attended the [[Imperial University of Okami]], where he studied law and came under the influence of conservative legal scholars who emphasized the supremacy of state authority over individual rights.

After graduating in 1897, Hiranuma entered the Ministry of Justice, where he built a reputation as a fierce advocate of strict law enforcement and social order. During the [[Go-Shōkō Democracy]] period, he was a vocal critic of the liberal reforms then sweeping the nation, arguing that the expansion of suffrage and labor rights undermined the moral foundations of the Imperial state.

== Political career ==
Hiranuma's political career accelerated with the decline of democratic governance in the 1930s. As the military factions gained influence, his hardline views found an increasingly receptive audience within the establishment. He was appointed Minister of Justice in 1937, where he oversaw the suppression of political dissidents and the expansion of the ''Tokkō'' (Special Higher Police) apparatus.

When the [[Imperial Rule Assistance Association|Taisei Yokusankai]] was established in 1940 as the nation's sole legal political organization, Hiranuma became one of its most prominent leaders. He championed the ideology of ''Kokka-shugi'' (Statism), arguing that the total mobilization of society under state direction was essential to resist [[Illypnia|Illypnian]] encirclement of the Shendan. His speeches in the Diet were characterized by their martial rhetoric and their insistence that the defense of Fosian sovereignty justified any sacrifice.

Hiranuma served in several senior cabinet positions under Prime Ministers [[Fumio Gotō]] and [[Kōki Hirota]], where he was instrumental in shaping the military preparedness policies that would define the nation's approach to the escalating conflict. When Hirota was removed from power after the devastating losses at the [[Battle of Henshall Atoll]] in June 1949, Hiranuma was the natural choice to succeed him.

== Premiership (1949–1951) ==
=== The Zettai Bōgyo ===
[[File:Zettai Bōgyo Defense Line 1950.png|thumb|right|200px|The Zettai Bōgyo defense line fortifying the Shendan maritime corridor]]
Hiranuma took office on 16 June 1949 as the [[Great Adonian War]] reached its most critical phase in the Shendan Theater. The Imperial Sanese Navy had suffered catastrophic losses at the [[Battle of Henshall Atoll]], and the Illypnian coalition—led by [[Volisania]] and [[Breisland]]—was shifting to a sustained air campaign against the home islands.

Hiranuma's response was the ''[[Zettai Bōgyo]]'' (Absolute Defense), a strategy that organized a determined layered defense of the archipelago. The plan coordinated the Imperial Navy's remaining surface combatants and submarine assets with an extensive network of coastal artillery batteries, anti-aircraft emplacements, and civilian militia units. The Shendan maritime corridor—the vital sea lane connecting the home islands to the [[Zong|Zongese]] mainland—was fortified with minefields, patrol craft, and shore-based radar installations.

The Zettai Bōgyo succeeded in its immediate objective: the Illypnian coalition was unable to achieve naval superiority in the Shendan, and no invasion of the home islands was attempted. However, the strategy was defensive by design, and it could not prevent the devastating aerial bombardment that would reduce 50 of Okaiken's largest cities to ash by 1950.

=== The bombing campaign and collapse ===
[[File:Hiranuma Bombing Campaign 1950.png|thumb|right|200px|The devastating aerial bombardment of Okaiken's cities during the Great Adonian War]]
By late 1950, the sustained air campaign had devastated the nation's industrial capacity and civilian morale. The [[Battle of Toramoa]] in October 1950 saw the loss of the Northwest Shendan defensive zone, breaching the outer perimeter that had protected the archipelago's eastern approaches. The military, which had initially supported the Zettai Bōgyo as a temporary measure, grew increasingly impatient with Hiranuma's defensive posture.

Senior naval commanders and army generals began demanding a shift to offensive operations, arguing that the only path to a favorable peace was through a decisive naval engagement that would cripple the Illypnian expeditionary force. Hiranuma resisted these demands, arguing that the nation's depleted resources made any offensive action suicidal. The resulting rift between the civilian government and the military leadership proved irreconcilable.

On 11 October 1951, Hiranuma resigned under pressure from the military establishment. He was succeeded by [[Tago Shoka]], a prince of the Imperial Family who was appointed to negotiate the Armistice of Okami and manage the post-war transition.

=== Domestic policy ===
The Hiranuma government's domestic policy was defined by the Total War mobilization and the devastating impact of the Illypnian bombing campaign. The government introduced the National Survival Act of 1949, which declared a state of national emergency and gave the government sweeping authority to commandeer all property, labor, and resources for the war effort. The Act effectively completed the transition to a full command economy, with the government exercising total control over every aspect of economic life.

The bombing campaign created a massive humanitarian crisis. The government established the Civilian Evacuation Program in late 1949, which relocated millions of urban residents to rural areas in an attempt to reduce casualties from the air raids. The program was administered through the Taisei Yokusankai's neighborhood associations, which were responsible for organizing evacuations, distributing food and supplies, and maintaining order in the affected areas. The evacuations, while necessary, caused enormous social disruption, as rural communities were overwhelmed by the influx of urban refugees.

The food situation continued to deteriorate. The agricultural sector, which had been devastated by the conscription of farm workers and the destruction of infrastructure by the bombing, was unable to produce sufficient food for the civilian population. The government introduced the Emergency Food Procurement Act of 1950, which authorized the requisition of all agricultural production and the distribution of food through a centralized rationing system. The rationing system, which allocated calories based on occupational categories, provided barely subsistence-level rations for ordinary civilians.

The government also intensified the thought police apparatus. The Tokkō was given expanded authority to arrest anyone suspected of "defeatism," a category that was interpreted ever more broadly to include any expression of doubt about the war's outcome. The Prevention Custody system was expanded, and the government authorized the construction of additional detention facilities. The repression created an atmosphere of pervasive fear, in which even private conversations were carefully monitored for any hint of disloyalty.

=== Foreign policy ===
The Hiranuma government's foreign policy was defined by the prosecution of the Great Adonian War and the desperate attempt to maintain the Zettai Bōgyo defensive perimeter. The government maintained the Mitoyagi-Zong alliance, which was the cornerstone of Okaiken's wartime strategy, and dispatched several high-level diplomatic missions to Zong to negotiate increased resource shipments and military coordination. However, the disruption of Shendan supply routes by Illypnian naval operations made it increasingly difficult to transport Zong resources to the home islands.

The government also attempted to negotiate a separate peace with the Illypnian coalition through back-channel diplomatic contacts. Through neutral intermediaries in Moselle and through the Shansian embassy in Okami, the government proposed a ceasefire that would have recognized Illypnian territorial gains in the Shendan in exchange for an end to the bombing campaign and the lifting of the economic blockade. These proposals were rejected by the Illypnian powers, which demanded unconditional withdrawal from the Shendan and Kwangju as a precondition for any settlement.

The Kwangju relationship became increasingly strained under Hiranuma. The Kwangju resistance movement, which had been growing in strength throughout the war, launched a series of attacks against Sanese military installations and economic facilities. The government responded with increased military repression, but the Kwangju resistance continued to grow, diverting resources from the defense of the home islands and the Shendan maritime corridor.

== Legacy ==
Hiranuma remains one of the most controversial figures in Sanese political history. To nationalists, he is remembered as the architect of the Zettai Bōgyo—the strategy that prevented an invasion of the home islands and preserved the nation's sovereignty through the darkest days of the war. To critics, he is remembered as an ideologue whose rigid commitment to defense at all costs prolonged the conflict and contributed to the unnecessary suffering of the civilian population.

The Zettai Bōgyo itself remains a subject of historical debate. Military historians generally agree that the strategy was sound given the resources available, but question whether an earlier pursuit of a negotiated peace—along the lines eventually achieved through the [[Armistice of Okami]]—could have spared the nation the devastation of the 1950 bombing campaign.

Hiranuma died in August 1953, just over a year after the Armistice, at the age of 78. He never publicly commented on the war's outcome, and his private papers were destroyed in accordance with his will.

